mruby gap: `# frozen_string_literal: true` is not implemented. The parser ignores
the magic comment, so every string literal is a fresh, unfrozen String. CRuby
freezes them and shares one object per content per VM, so `"abc".frozen?` is
true, a mutation raises FrozenError and two loads of the same literal are
`equal?`. Code written for that (and the bc2cpp frozen-literal lowering,
tools/bc2cpp/codegen_insn.rb) cannot be expressed on this mruby.

This patch, recorded as docs/adr/0546-persistent-frozen-string-literals.md:

  1. The lexer reads `frozen_string_literal: true|false` (case-insensitive,
     `-` or `_`) in the comment lines before the first token. A magic
     comment after code is ignored, as in CRuby.
  2. An all-literal string in such a file compiles to OP_FSTRING. The opcode
     is appended after STOP, so no existing opcode number moves: a file
     without the comment compiles to byte-identical bytecode, and existing
     .mrb files still load.
  3. OP_FSTRING returns mrb_frozen_literal(mrb, bytes): the VM's one frozen
     String for those bytes, kept in a hash on mrb_state that the GC marks.
     The hash is not a global variable, so `global_variables` does not list it.
  4. MRB_FL_OBJ_PERSISTENT marks a frozen String the sweep never frees before
     mrb_close and the mark does not traverse. mrb_obj_set_persistent refuses
     anything that could hold another collectable object: an unfrozen String,
     a String of another class, or one with a fshared buffer. A persistent
     String's singleton class is refused with FrozenError, since its class is
     not marked. The flag is type-qualified because no bit is free for every
     object type (classes use 17-19, envs 8-18).

